---
/**
 * Animation.
 *
 * The page exists mostly to explain an absence. There is no animation code in this library;
 * what there is instead is a clock port, two thresholds and a reduced-motion read that
 * defaults the right way round. Those three things are the whole subject.
 */
import DocPage from "../components/docs/DocPage.astro";
import Section from "../components/docs/Section.astro";
import Snippet from "../components/docs/Snippet.astro";
import Demo from "../components/docs/Demo.astro";
import Callout from "../components/docs/Callout.astro";
import PropsTable from "../components/docs/PropsTable.astro";
import { href } from "../lib/site";
import { ReducedMotionDemo } from "../components/demos/index.tsx";
import TimingTrace from "../components/TimingTrace.tsx";

const clockPortCode = `/** Timers and the current instant. */
export interface ClockPort {
  /** Monotonic milliseconds. Only ever used for differences, never as a wall clock. */
  readonly now: () => number;
  /** Runs fn after ms. Dispose to cancel. */
  readonly after: (ms: number, fn: () => void) => Disposer;
  /** Runs fn on each animation frame until disposed, with milliseconds since the call. */
  readonly frame: (fn: (elapsedMs: number) => void) => Disposer;
}`;

const reducedMotionCode = `// Nothing to enable. The hook reports the user's actual preference,
// and the library treats true as "reduce" everywhere without being asked.
const reduced = usePrefersReducedMotion();`;

const countdownCode = `const TICK_MS = 100;
const TICK_MS_REDUCED = 1000;

// Inside the frame subscription: re-render only when the bucket changes.
const granularity = reduced ? TICK_MS_REDUCED : TICK_MS;
const bucket = Math.floor(Math.max(0, targetAt - ports.clock.now()) / granularity);
if (bucket === lastBucket) return;`;

const thresholdCode = `// The defaults, written out.
const save = useAction(() => api.saveCustomer(form), {
  pending: { delay: 150, min: 400 },
});

// A dense table where every cell is a write: no delay, short hold.
const toggle = useAction(() => api.setFlag(row.id, next), {
  pending: { delay: 0, min: 200 },
});`;

const cssCode = `/* The library sets the attributes. The motion is yours. */

.btn {
  transition:
    opacity 120ms ease-out,
    border-color 120ms ease-out;
}

/* Present only once the delay threshold has passed, and held for the
   minimum duration. By the time this selector matches, the affordance
   has already earned its place: no CSS transition-delay needed. */
.btn[data-pending] {
  opacity: 0.72;
  cursor: progress;
}

/* aria-disabled, never disabled: the element is still focusable, so it
   still needs a focus ring. */
.btn[aria-disabled="true"]:focus-visible {
  outline: 2px solid var(--signal);
  outline-offset: 2px;
}

/* The full lifecycle, not a boolean. */
.btn[data-state="backoff"] {
  border-color: var(--warning);
}
.btn[data-state="error"] {
  border-color: var(--danger);
}
.btn[data-state="success"] {
  border-color: var(--good);
}`;

const spinnerCssCode = `.spinner {
  width: 0.85em;
  height: 0.85em;
  border: 2px solid currentColor;
  border-top-color: transparent;
  border-radius: 50%;
  animation: spin 700ms linear infinite;
}

@keyframes spin {
  to {
    transform: rotate(360deg);
  }
}

/* Reduction, done properly: the glyph stops turning and stays on screen.
   Hiding it would remove the information, which is not what the user asked for. */
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  .spinner {
    animation: none;
    border-top-color: currentColor;
    opacity: 0.45;
  }
}`;

const thresholds = [
  {
    name: "pending.delay",
    type: "number",
    default: "150",
    description:
      "Milliseconds of work before anything is shown. Below it, the action completes and the UI never changed.",
    why: "A 60ms save that flashes a spinner reads as a glitch, not as feedback. Most cached or local-network responses land under this line.",
  },
  {
    name: "pending.min",
    type: "number",
    default: "400",
    description: "Milliseconds the affordance stays once it has appeared, even if the work finished sooner.",
    why: "Without it, an action that resolves at 160ms shows a spinner for 10ms. That is a flicker, and a flicker reads as a bug rather than as progress.",
  },
];

const states = [
  {
    name: "busy",
    type: 'data-state="busy"',
    description:
      "Work is in flight. The affordance may or may not be visible yet: that is the delay threshold, tracked as context rather than as a separate state.",
  },
  {
    name: "settling",
    type: 'data-state="settling"',
    description:
      "The request has finished but the affordance has not been visible long enough. The result is held here until the minimum duration elapses.",
  },
  {
    name: "backoff",
    type: 'data-state="backoff"',
    description:
      'Waiting before an automatic retry. A real state rather than a gap, which is what lets a UI render "retrying in 3s" instead of appearing to hang.',
  },
];
---

<DocPage
  title="Animation"
  eyebrow="Concern"
  path="animation"
  lede="There is no animation library in here. There is one clock port, two thresholds and a reduced-motion preference that is respected without being asked. This page is about what that buys and what it leaves to you."
  meta={[
    { label: "Animation dependency", value: "none" },
    { label: "Runtime dependencies", value: "zero" },
    { label: "Countdown tick", value: "100ms, 1000ms reduced" },
  ]}
>
  <Section id="no-animation-library" title="There is no animation library here">
    <p>
      Nothing in this library animates anything. No spring, no tween, no transform, no keyframes, no layout
      projection. Both packages have zero runtime dependencies, so there is no animation library in the graph
      either, and nothing to tree-shake out of it.
    </p>
    <p>
      What exists instead is the clock port. Every timed behaviour in the library &mdash; the pending delay,
      the minimum display duration, the retry backoff, the live countdown &mdash; goes through it, and nothing
      captures a global <code>setTimeout</code> or
      <code>requestAnimationFrame</code> at module scope.
    </p>

    <Snippet code={clockPortCode} lang="ts" name="packages/core/src/ports.ts" />

    <p>
      <code>frame</code> is the only continuous thing in the library, and one shared loop drives every live countdown
      through it. A component with no countdown pending holds no subscription at all: the effect returns early when
      there is no target, so an idle trigger costs nothing per frame.
    </p>
    <p>Three things follow from the port being an interface rather than a global.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Timing is deterministic in tests.</strong> A fake clock replaces the real one, so a 150&thinsp;ms
        threshold is asserted by advancing a counter instead of sleeping. The suite does not flake, which is the
        only reason timing this precise is maintainable at all.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>It degrades rather than throwing.</strong> In a runtime with no animation frame source, core falls
        back to a 100&thinsp;ms interval, so a countdown still advances in Node or in a worker.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The adapter can downgrade it.</strong> Consumers read a value, not a frame rate, which is what makes
        the reduced-motion behaviour below possible without a second code path.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>What it costs</h3>
    <p>
      Nothing, which is the point. <code>@unruffled/react</code> and
      <code>@unruffled/core</code> together are 7.08&thinsp;kB brotli with everything retained: the React layer
      is 2.07&thinsp;kB of that, the core barrel 4.83&thinsp;kB, the machine runtime 1.49&thinsp;kB and the ports
      454&thinsp;B. A spring solver is not in the budget because it is not in the library.
    </p>
    <Callout tone="note" title="Bring your own motion">
      <p>
        If you want springs, use a motion library. This one sets attributes and hands you the timing; it has
        no opinion on your easing curve and does not compete for the job. The
        <a href={href("theming")}>theming page</a> covers the attribute surface in full.
      </p>
    </Callout>
  </Section>

  <Section id="reduced-motion" title="Reduced motion is the default">
    <p>
      <code>usePrefersReducedMotion</code> reports the user's preference, and the library treats
      <code>true</code> as "reduce" everywhere. There is no flag to turn that on.
    </p>
    <Snippet code={reducedMotionCode} name="reduced motion" />
    <p>
      State that plainly against the alternative: the dominant animation library ships with reduction switched <em
        >off</em
      > and requires an explicit opt-in that most applications never discover. An accessibility default that has
      to be found in the documentation is not a default; it is a feature with a discovery problem, and the users
      it exists for are the ones who lose.
    </p>
    <p>Two further defects are common in this exact hook, and both are avoided here.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>It is reactive.</strong> The widespread implementation reads
        <code>matchMedia</code> once into state at mount. A user who turns the preference on mid-session, or who
        drags the window to a display with different settings, keeps getting animation until a remount. This subscribes
        to the media query, so the answer updates.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>It is server-safe.</strong> Without <code>matchMedia</code> the answer is
        <code>false</code>, which renders the motion-enabled markup and corrects itself on hydration. That
        ordering is deliberate: it keeps the server output stable rather than guessing at a preference it
        cannot read.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <Demo
      title="The preference, read live"
      hint="Change it in your OS settings with this page open"
      note="No reload. The value, the countdown granularity and the spinner all update from the media-query subscription."
    >
      <ReducedMotionDemo client:visible />
    </Demo>
  </Section>

  <Section id="amplitude" title="Reduction means amplitude, not a kill switch">
    <p>
      The common reading of <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code> is "remove the moving thing". For a progress affordance
      that is wrong, because the moving thing is carrying information.
    </p>
    <p>
      Take the retry countdown. It is the one piece of UI a rate-limited user actually needs: how long until
      this can work. "Snap instantly" and "animate fully" are both wrong answers. So under reduction the
      countdown keeps counting and stays accurate &mdash; the bucket simply widens from 100&thinsp;ms to
      1000&thinsp;ms, so the number updates once a second instead of ten times a second. It stops animating.
      It does not stop informing.
    </p>

    <Snippet code={countdownCode} lang="ts" name="packages/react/src/adapter/useCountdown.ts" />

    <p>Applied consistently, that gives three rules worth copying into your own CSS.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>A spinner stops turning. It stays on screen.</li>
      <li>A countdown slows its tick. It keeps the correct number.</li>
      <li>A state change loses its transition. It does not lose its colour or its label.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Freezing or hiding the countdown would withhold the one thing the user asked about, in the name of a
      preference about motion. Reduction is an amplitude control, not a mute button.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="thresholds" title="The thresholds as motion design">
    <p>
      A spinner is normally discussed as a styling question. It is a timing question, and two numbers decide
      almost all of it.
    </p>
    <PropsTable rows={thresholds} nameHeading="Threshold" />
    <p>
      Together they define a window in which nothing happens visually, and a floor under how briefly anything
      can appear. Under 150&thinsp;ms the user gets their result and the interface never moved, which is the
      best possible outcome and the one a boolean
      <code>isPending</code> cannot produce. Over it, whatever appeared is guaranteed at least 400&thinsp;ms on
      screen, which is long enough to be perceived as feedback rather than noticed as a glitch.
    </p>

    <Demo
      title="Where the thresholds fall"
      hint="Press it, then try it again at a different latency"
      note="The trace is real: it samples the machine through the clock port while the request runs. 700ms latency against a 150ms delay and a 400ms minimum."
    >
      <TimingTrace
        client:visible
        latencyMs={700}
        windowMs={1200}
        pendingDelayMs={150}
        pendingMinMs={400}
        buttonLabel="Save customer"
      />
    </Demo>

    <p>
      This needs more than a boolean in the machine, because "work is in flight" and "the affordance is on
      screen" come apart in both directions. Three of the six states exist for exactly that.
    </p>
    <PropsTable rows={states} nameHeading="State" />
    <p>
      From the user's point of view none of the three has finished, so
      <code>status</code> reports all of them as <code>pending</code> and
      <code>aria-busy</code> is true throughout. <code>data-state</code> gives you the raw value when you want to
      style the difference.
    </p>

    <Snippet code={thresholdCode} name="overriding the thresholds" />

    <Callout tone="warning" title="Zero delay is a choice, not a shortcut">
      <p>
        <code>delay: 0</code> shows the affordance immediately, which is right for a dense table of instant toggles
        where the row needs to look touched. It also brings back the flash on a fast response. Pair it with a short
        <code>min</code> rather than dropping both.
      </p>
    </Callout>
  </Section>

  <Section id="what-you-animate" title="What you animate yourself">
    <p>
      The library's entire contribution to your visuals is a set of attributes on your element. There is no
      class name to match, no stylesheet to import, and no wrapper element.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>data-pending</code> &mdash; present, with an empty value, while the affordance should render.
      </li>
      <li><code>data-state</code> &mdash; the raw machine state, one of the six.</li>
      <li><code>data-attempt</code> &mdash; the attempt number from the second attempt onwards.</li>
      <li>
        <code>aria-busy</code> and <code>aria-disabled</code> &mdash; the accessibility surface, which doubles as
        a selector.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <Snippet code={cssCode} lang="css" name="your stylesheet" />

    <p>
      Note what is absent from that CSS: a <code>transition-delay</code> to stop the spinner flashing. You do not
      need one, because by the time
      <code>[data-pending]</code> matches, the 150&thinsp;ms threshold has already passed and the result is already
      held for 400&thinsp;ms. The timing is in the machine, so the CSS can be naive. That is the division of labour
      the library is arguing for.
    </p>
    <p>
      The one rule to carry into your own motion: honour the preference in CSS too. The library handles its
      own timing, but your keyframes are yours.
    </p>

    <Snippet code={spinnerCssCode} lang="css" name="your spinner" />
  </Section>

  <Section id="performance" title="Performance">
    <p>
      A live countdown is the easiest way to accidentally re-render a tree sixty times a second. Three
      decisions keep it cheap.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Renders are bucketed.</strong> The frame callback computes which 100&thinsp;ms bucket the remaining
        time falls in and returns without doing anything if the bucket has not changed. A number that visibly changes
        ten times a second costs about ten renders a second, not sixty. Under reduced motion the bucket is 1000&thinsp;ms,
        so roughly one.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The subscription exists only while a countdown is pending.</strong> With no target there is no frame
        subscription, so an idle or settled action is not in the loop at all. A table of two hundred rows with nothing
        retrying holds zero frame callbacks.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>State is a tick counter, not the value.</strong> The remaining time is computed during render, where
        it is current by construction. The only <code>setState</code> is in the frame callback. Storing the value
        in state would mean writing it from an effect and cascading a second render on every update.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The machine itself holds no timers it is not using. Each timed behaviour is an effect of the state that
      needs it, so leaving a state disposes its timer: <code>busy</code> owns the request and the delay timer, <code
        >settling</code
      > owns the hold timer,
      <code>backoff</code> owns the retry timer. The fake clock exposes
      <code>pending()</code> and <code>frames()</code> so the suite can assert that a torn-down machine leaves zero
      of each, which is how that claim stays true rather than becoming aspirational.
    </p>
    <p>
      One more consequence of the port: the effect that owns the request is scoped to the
      <code>busy</code> state, and disposing it aborts. An unmount mid-request tears the request down rather than
      leaving it to resolve into a component that is gone.
    </p>
  </Section>
</DocPage>
